 ],
 "literal": "Hare-leaf plant's blooming beautiful flowers having crushed, fish eating / broad-legged heron staying on the sand dune / of the cool shore's lord, holding onto, our beauty / \"we will take back,\" you say, O friend! We will take back, / fearing distress, those who begged for what they desired, / \"give back those things given!\" even if one says, / is it not more painful than the loss of our sweet life?",
 "translation": "\"O friend, you say we should go to the lord of the cool shore—where the broad-legged heron stands on the sand dunes, eating fish after crushing the beautiful, blooming flowers of the adumbu vine—and demand back our beauty that we lost to him. Even if one were to ask those who begged out of distress to return the gifts they were given, would that not be more painful than losing our own sweet life?\"",
 "modern": "அடும்புக்கொடியின் மலர்ந்த அழகிய பூக்களை மிதித்துச் சிதைத்துவிட்டு, மீன்களை இரையாக உண்ணும் அகன்ற கால்களைக் கொண்ட நாரை வாழ்கின்ற மணல் மேடுகள் நிறைந்த குளிர்ந்த கடற்கரைத் தலைவனைப் பற்றிக் கூறி, அவனிடம் இழந்த நம் அழகை நாம் திரும்பப் பெற்றுக்கொள்வோம் என்று கூறுகிறாய் தோழி! வறுமைக்கு அஞ்சித் தன்னிடம் இரந்து கேட்டவர்களுக்குக் கொடுத்த பொருள்களை, 'நான் கொடுத்ததைத் திரும்பக் கொடு' என்று கேட்பது, நம்முடைய இனிய உயிரை இழப்பதை விடவும் துன்பம் தருவது அல்லவா?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante in a coastal (neytal) setting. The confidante has suggested that they should confront the hero, who has neglected the heroine, and demand back the beauty and health she lost because of her pining. The heroine rejects this idea, comparing it to a donor shamelessly asking a beggar to return the gifts they once gave out of charity. She feels that demanding back her love or beauty from the hero would be more humiliating and painful than death itself. The image of the heron crushing the beautiful adumbu flowers to eat fish represents the hero's careless behavior, as he destroys her beauty and happiness for his own passing pleasures.",
